WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.780
A library may receive data from files, network responses, plugins, IPC messages, UI input, configuration, or other libraries.

2
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:19.240
Even when the library is not directly exposed to a remote client, boundary data should be treated as untrusted until it is validated against the library contract.

3
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.860
Consumers often reuse libraries in contexts the original author did not anticipate.

4
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.860
Validation should happen before sensitive use.

5
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:40.040
Lengths, numeric ranges, encodings, required fields, object relationships, and structural assumptions should be checked before allocation, parsing, indexing, or state mutation.

6
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:48.780
Parsers should have resource limits so malformed or oversized input cannot consume unbounded memory, time, or nested structure depth.

7
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.880
Validation rules should be documented so callers know what the library accepts and rejects.

8
00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.500
This is especially important when the same parser is reused in tools, services, and desktop applications.

9
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.460
Error handling is part of the API contract.

10
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:11.840
Exceptions, status objects, error codes, and result types can all be valid choices when used consistently.

11
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:25.220
The important thing is that callers can reliably distinguish success, invalid input, unsupported features, resource exhaustion, and internal failure without scraping unstable message text.

12
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.720
Consistency matters more than style preference because callers build control flow around failure signals.

13
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.560
Failure should preserve invariants.

14
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.420
A failed parse should not leave half-mutated global state.

15
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.640
A rejected configuration should not partially apply.

16
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:48.700
Cleanup should be predictable, and errors should provide enough information for safe response without exposing sensitive internals.

17
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.840
Secure libraries fail in ways that callers can understand, test, and recover from.

18
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.980
Negative tests should verify those failure contracts just as carefully as successful cases.

19
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:06.920
Callers should not need to guess whether retry, cleanup, or replacement is required afterward in each supported build mode and binding layer.
